  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "None; the full paper and supplementary material are present.",
    "relation": "tests",
    "tranche": "T3",
    "tranche_reason": "The study evaluates downstream PTSD-severity prediction across model sizes, using correlation and absolute error rather than held-out pretraining loss or controlled parameter/token/compute scaling experiments.",
    "gate_note": "Rows cover the model-size evaluation of PTSD-severity prediction against self-reported PCL scores. The paper also reports many other model, prompt, reasoning-effort, calibration, ensemble, and clinical-validity results; these do not test the listed scaling-law versions. It cites scaling laws from prior work, which are not recorded as results here.",
    "test_reason": "The study compares one downstream task (PTSD-severity estimation from interview text) across sizes of one family, LLaMA-3.1-Instruct at 8B, 70B and 405B. It also compares DeepSeek-Distil-LLaMA at 8B and 70B. This is a new setting with a result on V6. There are only two or three size points, no fitted curve and no pretraining loss, so it bears only on whether downstream accuracy improves monotonically with size.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly correct. The authors say accuracy generally improves with size but plateaus beyond 70B for open-weight models (LLaMA, DeepSeek). They call this a saturation point and suggest the scaling laws observed elsewhere may not hold for complex clinical estimation. They also report that closed-weight models improve with newer generations, with GPT-5 best, but those sizes are undisclosed.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "This study compares downstream PTSD-severity prediction across pretrained model sizes using correlation and MAE. It does not measure held-out pretraining cross-entropy or run controlled parameter, token, and compute sweeps.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Model size and prompting strategy (0-shot versus 3-shot) change results; the paper reports that few-shot effects are inconsistent and can degrade performance.",
    "fits_no_version": [],
    "controls": "y",
    "capability_check": "y",
    "uncertainty_reported": "y",
    "code_released": "not stated; the paper says the code will be made available through a GitHub repository but gives no URL.",
    "peer_reviewed": "preprint",
    "problems": "The size comparison is across different model families and variants, not a controlled sweep of a single training recipe. Model sizes are undisclosed for some systems. The reported downstream task metrics do not measure pretraining loss, and the paper does not fit a scaling curve. Temperature was fixed at 0.0, so repeated decoding variability was not measured.",
    "rows_left_out": "Other model families and model configurations, including base models and additional prompting variants, were omitted to keep the record focused on the size comparisons bearing on downstream predictability. Results are in Table S7. Metrics for selected model-size conditions are retained separately for Pearson correlation and MAE because the study reports both.",
    "untested": [
